From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 23:47:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: let a lone host DP tunnel run at HBR3, two share
 HBR2

TB_HOST_DP_HBR2_MAX caps every DP tunnel from the host router's DP IN
adapters at HBR2, because both DP IN streams of the PHY must run at one
link rate. A single display does not need that cap: alone on the host
router a DP tunnel can run at HBR3 x4 (25920 Mb/s), which carries modes
that HBR2 x4 (17280 Mb/s) cannot.

Rename the flag to TB_HOST_DP_SHARED_RATE and decide the cap where DP IN
and DP OUT adapters are paired, under the domain lock:

- when one DP tunnel will be up on the host router, it may offer up to
  HBR3; when more will, each new one offers HBR2 at most;
- when another DP tunnel is wanted (a second DP OUT adapter became
  available) while one is up above HBR2, that one is torn down and set up
  again between the same adapters at HBR2 first; the new one then takes
  its rate. Its display sees an unplug and a plug. If it cannot be set up
  again, pairing runs once more, and at the next event;
- a tunnel alone that has to be reduced takes the combination with the
  most bandwidth (HBR2 x4 rather than HBR3 x2). Held at HBR2, or next to
  another tunnel, it keeps the rate and gives up lanes as before.

A host DP tunnel is also counted at its cap at most in the bandwidth
left for the others. Once its DPRX is done, the bandwidth it takes is
worked out from what its DP IN adapter reports, which the cap does not
bound: held at HBR2 between adapters that take HBR3, it could be counted
at HBR3 x4, and a second display next to it would then get HBR2 x2
rather than x4.

Nothing is set up again during a system sleep transition: from its
PREPARE event (a PM notifier ahead of all others) until it is over, POST
callbacks and the rollback of a refused PREPARE included (the gate is
reopened by a work item that takes the system sleep lock after the POST
event); a re-link admitted before holds the transition back until it is
done. The re-link, and the pairing that needs it, then wait:
a work item on the freezable workqueue hands them to the domain's ordered
workqueue once the transition is over, behind any DPRX or teardown work
queued before. The request also stays pending for the resume and thaw
replays, and a failed runtime resume of the domain is retried a few
times.

The KUnit tests reach the connection manager's DP bandwidth accounting
on a domain set up without hardware, reading adapter registers through
a static stub on tb_cfg_read(); tb_cm_init() is split out of
tb_probe() for that. The DP repair host test models the connection
manager's private data: add the new work item there, and take the work
initializers from tb_cm_init().

Signed-off-by: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>

(backported from commit 39391bb8b596aface4d5126b92c78c594b5cbd11)
[j604: this tree's tb_dp_tunnel_active() keeps its one-argument form
 (the callback without a data pointer); only the forward declaration of
 tb_queue_dp_tunnels() is added next to it.]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
